00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.490
MAYANK TAHILRAMANI: Brilliant.

00:00:57.490 --> 00:00:59.673
So before we dive into
the joint solution,

00:00:59.673 --> 00:01:01.090
let's talk about
the core building

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.390
blocks of what we're discussing.

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.498
Give a little bit of
context and background.

00:01:06.498 --> 00:01:08.290
Particularly around
the core building block

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:12.040
of Kubernetes as a robust
platform and service

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:14.320
to manage all your
container workloads.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:16.750
As companies look to move
into microservice space

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.180
for architecture and
containerized or application,

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.770
Kubernetes becomes the de
facto platform to deploy to.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.920
And as organizations grow
and complexity arises,

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.680
you can imagine more and
more Kubernetes clusters

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:31.780
across hybrid or
multicloud environments

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:33.500
become the de facto norm.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.470
So to manage that
complexity, whether it's

00:01:35.470 --> 00:01:38.020
across policy management,
networking, managing

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:42.220
different clusters, all of that
complexity can be addressed

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.140
with Anthos as a solution.

00:01:45.140 --> 00:01:47.290
Anthos is a unified platform.

00:01:47.290 --> 00:01:50.680
It's managed applicable
for multi and hybrid cloud

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.670
environments that are
running Kubernetes workloads.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.420
Particularly with
Anthos Config Management

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.460
that we'll be focusing
on today's talk

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.770
on how you can manage those
policies and declarative

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:03.070
code to manage complexity
across your different clusters

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:04.360
and environments.

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.850
On top Anthos, it
is GitLab which

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:08.800
helps you provide that
workflow management

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:10.925
capability in a
single application

00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:12.425
for your operators,
your developers,

00:02:12.425 --> 00:02:13.752
for your security engineers.

00:02:13.752 --> 00:02:15.460
A single pane of glass
where they can all

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.270
work, store their code,
and provide information

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:22.870
concurrently and collaboratively
as a single source of truth.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.210
A single DevOps application
that can help you

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.910
manage how you
operate and deploy to

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:31.960
across Kubernetes
clusters using Anthos.

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:34.760
But that's the
technology aspect of it.

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.480
Let's also talk about the
cultural aspect of it.

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.690
Why even go down the route of
declarative infrastructure,

00:02:40.690 --> 00:02:44.770
declarative code to
operate your complexity?

00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:46.090
Why GitOps in general?

00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:48.400
Really with GitOps, you
get a lot of reliability.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.370
Once you've defined it and
fully automated the process,

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.850
you're able to replicate
it in a reliable fashion.

00:02:54.850 --> 00:02:56.830
The more your software
defines your operation,

00:02:56.830 --> 00:02:58.705
the more your software
defines your business,

00:02:58.705 --> 00:03:01.390
the less human intervention
that you have to work with.

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:04.450
You abstract and
allow humans to focus

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.160
on the intellectual
contributions

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.980
to your iterations and
improvements versus having

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.872
error-prone processes,
where different teams

00:03:10.872 --> 00:03:12.580
are having to communicate
with each other

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:16.660
to asynchronous or different
means that are not automated.

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.570
Accessibility becomes also
another great value proposition

00:03:19.570 --> 00:03:20.830
of GitOps.

00:03:20.830 --> 00:03:22.398
Once it's in Git,
it's versioned.

00:03:22.398 --> 00:03:23.440
It's the source of truth.

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:24.850
You can track the change.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.730
You can concurrently collaborate
as things are being ported,

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:28.780
changed, and redeployed.

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:31.360
You can track those
changes over time

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:33.080
using Git as the
fundamental protocol

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:36.328
and then using GitLab for
the workflow aspect of it.

00:03:36.328 --> 00:03:38.620
At the end of the day, you're
using familiar tools when

00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.090
you're trying to adopt GitOps.

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:41.920
You're using
Kubernetes manifest.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.170
You're using YAML.

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:45.550
That's much more
declarative and explicit.

00:03:45.550 --> 00:03:48.880
You're using CI/CD and
merge requests in GitLab.

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:51.130
And you're using Git,
allowing more user personas

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:54.100
in a single pane of glass
to operate familiar tools

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:57.290
that developers are already
familiar with today.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:00.530
So let's now dive into the
reference architecture.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:03.100
How do we configure GitLab
and your repositories

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:05.290
to work better,
to work seamlessly

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:07.130
with Anthos Config Management?

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:09.670
Theo, do you mind walking
us through this slide?

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:10.730
THEO CHAMLEY: Yeah, sure.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:11.810
Thanks, Mayank.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.840
So really, this is our
reference architecture.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.570
On the right, you have two
Google Kubernetes staging, one

00:04:17.570 --> 00:04:19.550
staging and one for production.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.360
You, of course, can
have more than that,

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.420
but that's just our example.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.420
And in the center,
here in that gray box,

00:04:26.420 --> 00:04:29.630
we have the core of the
system, which is GitLab.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:32.430
And we are using GitLab to
host three different Git

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:33.820
repositories.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:35.660
And the first one that
I want to talk about

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:38.560
is the one in the middle
with the black logo.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.663
It's called Anthos
Config Management.

00:04:40.663 --> 00:04:42.080
And that's a Git
repository that's

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:45.590
going to be mainly used
by the platform teams.

00:04:45.590 --> 00:04:48.290
And the goal of Anthos
Config Management

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:52.910
is to bring a GitOps approach
to configure the policies.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:55.790
So with that repository,
when you commit a config

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:59.960
to that repository, you will
be able to automatically apply

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.600
that configuration or that
policy across all your clusters

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:03.848
at once.

00:05:03.848 --> 00:05:05.640
Anthosd because it's
in a GitLab repository

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:09.320
and because it's declarative,
you can use all the usual Git

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.300
workflows and GitLab
workflows that you're

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.700
used to, such as merge
request, or approvals,

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.960
or CI tests, et cetera,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:21.020
And on the left, we have
two other Git repositories

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.420
that are used for the last
component of this reference

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:24.410
architecture.

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:27.320
This last component is
application delivery manager,

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:31.140
or appctrl for short, which
is the name of the CLI.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.960
And so we had Anthos
Config Management.

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:35.930
That's GitOps for
config and policies.

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:36.990
And we have appctl.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.440
That's GitOps for applications.

00:05:39.440 --> 00:05:41.630
So the first of those
two Git repositories--

00:05:41.630 --> 00:05:43.200
that's the one
with the red logo--

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:45.110
that's the application
repository.

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:48.182
And that's really a normal
application repository.

00:05:48.182 --> 00:05:49.640
In our case, it's
called HelloWorld

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.620
because I'm a fairly
bad developer.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.350
But it's really a
normal Git repository,

00:05:54.350 --> 00:05:57.530
where you have your source
code, be it Java, Python, Ruby,

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:58.760
whatever you want.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:06:01.580
The only difference is
that you have basically

00:06:01.580 --> 00:06:04.400
templates for your
Kubernetes configurations

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:06.390
in that repository.

00:06:06.390 --> 00:06:09.250
And in that repository, you
work as you would normally.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:12.290
And the last repository is
the one with the yellow logo.

00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:15.230
It's called
deployment repository.

00:06:15.230 --> 00:06:17.930
And that one is mainly
managed by appctl.

00:06:17.930 --> 00:06:19.790
You don't have to
interact with it too much.

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:21.650
And the only thing
that it contains

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:25.730
is the fully built
Kubernetes configurations

00:06:25.730 --> 00:06:28.280
that are built from
the templates that

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:33.120
are in the first repository,
the application repository.

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:37.100
And so that last repository,
the deployment one,

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:39.170
contains those
full configurations

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:40.790
for each environment.

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:43.540
And appctl is going
to apply those as-is

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:47.090
to your different
Kubernetes clusters.

00:06:47.090 --> 00:06:50.540
So let's deep dive into the
first of those components

00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:52.980
that I mentioned, Anthos
Config Management.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:56.540
So just to preface everything,
Anthos Config Management

00:06:56.540 --> 00:07:00.350
is part of Anthos and is a
globally available product

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:01.220
today.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:04.520
So it actually has three
different subcomponents.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:06.830
The first of those is
called config sync.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:11.110
And config sync is taking the
configurations and policies

00:07:11.110 --> 00:07:13.790
and everything that you
have in your Git repository

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:17.600
and applying that to
your Kubernetes clusters.

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:19.820
And really, it can be
any common subjects.

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:22.760
But we are focusing on config
and policies and things

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:28.790
like [INAUDIBLE] or [INAUDIBLE]
or network policies,

00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:30.592
those kinds of things.

00:07:30.592 --> 00:07:33.050
The second of those components
is called policy controller.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:36.440
And that's a managed version of
an open-source project called

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.870
Open Policy Agent Gatekeeper.

00:07:38.870 --> 00:07:42.560
And policy controller allows
you to enforce policies

00:07:42.560 --> 00:07:43.805
across all of your clusters.

00:07:43.805 --> 00:07:46.790
So for example, if you
want all your deployments

00:07:46.790 --> 00:07:50.000
to be labeled with
an Owner label,

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:53.760
allowing you to identify which
team owns which deployment,

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.650
then you can enforce that
with policy controller.

00:07:57.650 --> 00:08:01.490
It was declared the policy
in your Git repository.

00:08:01.490 --> 00:08:02.990
And every time
that the deployment

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:05.410
is going to be created,
policy controller

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:08.270
is going to allow
that creation on it

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:10.970
depending on what you
have in your deployment.

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:14.190
And the last component that
I'm not going to demo today

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.620
is called config connector.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:20.390
And that makes the link between
Kubernetes custom resources

00:08:20.390 --> 00:08:21.810
and GCP resources.

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:25.280
So for example, you can
have a Cloud SQL custom

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:26.305
resource in Kubernetes.

00:08:26.305 --> 00:08:27.680
And the config
connector is going

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:31.940
to create and manage for you
the corresponding Cloud SQL

00:08:31.940 --> 00:08:33.679
instance in GCP.

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:37.820
And between config sync
and config connector,

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:39.860
you can declare
your GCP resources

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:42.919
in your Git repository and
have them created and managed

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:45.140
in GCP.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:47.160
So let's move to
the next component,

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.270
which is application
delivery, AKA appctl.

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:53.195
So appctl is not
yet a GA product.

00:08:53.195 --> 00:08:54.470
It's prerelease.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:55.730
It's freely available.

00:08:55.730 --> 00:08:59.780
And we are actively looking for
user feedback on that product

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:07.320
to make it even better and even
more flexible for the users.

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:10.070
So as I mentioned
previously, appctl really

00:09:10.070 --> 00:09:11.480
works with two repositories.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:13.530
On the left here
with the red logo,

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.290
we have the
application repository.

00:09:15.290 --> 00:09:17.840
And it's really a normal
application repository,

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:19.530
where you're going to develop.

00:09:19.530 --> 00:09:22.930
You're going to create commits,
create branches, create merge

00:09:22.930 --> 00:09:25.288
requests in GitLab,
and have a peer

00:09:25.288 --> 00:09:27.830
review on that merge request,
[INAUDIBLE] CI test, et cetera,

00:09:27.830 --> 00:09:28.820
et cetera.

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:32.210
And once that merge
request is approved,

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:35.380
you're going to merge it
back on the main bunch.

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:37.940
It's called master here.

00:09:37.940 --> 00:09:40.790
And once you want to
release your application

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:42.650
to your various
environments, you're

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:47.690
going to tag a specific
commit-- in our case, 1.2.3.

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:50.960
At that stage, you can
handle the common appctl

00:09:50.960 --> 00:09:52.220
prepare staging.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:53.720
And what that does--

00:09:53.720 --> 00:09:56.210
looks at your
application repository,

00:09:56.210 --> 00:10:01.430
takes the last state of that,
and builds the full Kubernetes

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:06.040
configurations for that
version of your application.

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:08.500
And create a merge request
in the deployment repository

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.250
on the right here
with the yellow logo.

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:15.490
We have that full
Kubernetes configuration.

00:10:15.490 --> 00:10:18.010
And because it's a merge
request, you can review it.

00:10:18.010 --> 00:10:22.840
You can run a CI test in GitLab
here on that merge request,

00:10:22.840 --> 00:10:24.460
all the normal things.

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:27.220
And really, it allows you
to see exactly what's going

00:10:27.220 --> 00:10:29.400
to be deployed in staging.

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:31.810
And once you're fine
with that, you actually

00:10:31.810 --> 00:10:34.390
merge that onto
the staging branch.

00:10:34.390 --> 00:10:36.593
And you run appctl
apply staging.

00:10:36.593 --> 00:10:38.760
And that's actually going
to deploy your application

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:41.680
in staging.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:43.630
And you can see that
in that repository.

00:10:43.630 --> 00:10:45.290
On the deployment
one on the right,

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:46.600
we have multiple branches.

00:10:46.600 --> 00:10:49.223
And we have actually one
branch per environment-- one

00:10:49.223 --> 00:10:51.640
for the staging environment,
one for the prod environment,

00:10:51.640 --> 00:10:55.360
and one for other environments
if you have other environments.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:57.520
And the last thing
that we want to do

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:01.510
is promote our application
from the staging environment

00:11:01.510 --> 00:11:03.350
to the production environment.

00:11:03.350 --> 00:11:06.220
And for that, we are going to
run the command appctl prepare

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:08.560
prod from-env staging.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:10.420
And appctl is going
to look at the version

00:11:10.420 --> 00:11:12.790
that you currently
have in staging,

00:11:12.790 --> 00:11:14.680
build the full
Kubernetes configurations

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:19.510
for prod, but with the
version used in staging,

00:11:19.510 --> 00:11:21.675
and create a match request
for the prod branch

00:11:21.675 --> 00:11:23.550
that you're going to be
able to review, test,

00:11:23.550 --> 00:11:24.940
et cetera, et cetera.

00:11:24.940 --> 00:11:27.370
And once you merge
that merge request,

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:29.530
you can run appctl apply prod.

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:31.660
And that's going to
deploy your application

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:33.970
in production with
the same version

00:11:33.970 --> 00:11:35.380
that you had in staging.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:38.530
So I know that all of
that is a lot to take in.

00:11:38.530 --> 00:11:42.220
So I think it's better
to look at a demo

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:47.200
and to see how everything
works together in reality.

00:11:47.200 --> 00:11:49.890
We are starting with
two GKE clusters, one

00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:51.900
for the production
environment and one

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:54.770
for the staging environment.

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:57.430
As you can see, I have
access to my staging cluster

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:00.750
in that case with three nodes.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:04.790
This is the Anthos Config
Management Git repository.

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:08.850
And in this repository, I can
define the config policies.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:11.880
In this particular
directory, for example,

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:16.680
I can list my clusters
and give them labels.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.240
This is also where I'm going
to define my namespaces.

00:12:19.240 --> 00:12:22.740
In our case, I'm defining
an HelloWorld namespace.

00:12:22.740 --> 00:12:25.410
Just putting that
namespace in that file

00:12:25.410 --> 00:12:29.260
makes it appear in my
cluster, as you can see here.

00:12:29.260 --> 00:12:33.600
The HelloWorld namespace
already exists in my cluster.

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:36.250
In that repository,
I already have

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:40.170
merge request created
for this demo.

00:12:40.170 --> 00:12:42.480
And this merge request
is going to allow

00:12:42.480 --> 00:12:46.900
me to modify configurations
and policies on that cluster.

00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:49.230
So for example here,
the merge request

00:12:49.230 --> 00:12:51.900
is designed to add a
constraint to policy

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:55.950
that says that every deployment
in the namespace HelloWorld

00:12:55.950 --> 00:13:01.530
and SEN tests need to
have a label called team.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:04.500
And for that merge request,
because it's GitLab,

00:13:04.500 --> 00:13:08.015
I can just have a
GitLab CI pipeline.

00:13:08.015 --> 00:13:09.390
And here, I have
a couple of jobs

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:12.445
that [INAUDIBLE] that my
configuration [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:12.445 --> 00:13:13.590
is valid.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:16.560
And also that validates
that my own configuration

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:22.110
is compliant with the
policies that I set.

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:23.540
To change policies,
of course, you

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:26.082
want to make sure that everyone
who needs to approve a policy

00:13:26.082 --> 00:13:27.700
change is going to approve it.

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:31.560
So in that case, we are using
the approval feature of GitLab

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:32.620
to make sure of that.

00:13:32.620 --> 00:13:34.290
And in that case,
I just gave myself

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:35.670
the approval permissions.

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:37.690
So we are going to merge that.

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:41.150
And you're going to see that our
new policy is going to appear

00:13:41.150 --> 00:13:42.450
in the cluster fairly quickly.

00:13:45.258 --> 00:13:47.520
And here it is.

00:13:47.520 --> 00:13:50.952
So until now, I was playing the
role of a platform [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:50.952 --> 00:13:51.660
Now let's switch.

00:13:51.660 --> 00:13:53.810
And let's say that
I'm a developer.

00:13:53.810 --> 00:13:55.860
I have this very simple
hello-world application

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:57.540
that just displays
"Hello, world"

00:13:57.540 --> 00:13:59.580
potentially with
a custom message.

00:13:59.580 --> 00:14:00.960
And it's written in Python.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.730
And I have [INAUDIBLE]--

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:04.800
everything very normal.

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:10.132
And I have a CI pipeline
for that application.

00:14:13.230 --> 00:14:16.690
And I'm going to use appctl
to deploy that application.

00:14:16.690 --> 00:14:20.340
So I initialize my
project with appctl.

00:14:20.340 --> 00:14:22.080
And it's actually
going to create

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:24.780
a new project, HelloWorld
deployment that's

00:14:24.780 --> 00:14:26.760
going to contain my
configurations that we're

00:14:26.760 --> 00:14:29.940
going to deploy to my clusters.

00:14:29.940 --> 00:14:32.430
And in my project
now, you can see

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:37.142
that I have two new directories,
one called Delivery and one

00:14:37.142 --> 00:14:37.725
called Config.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:44.190
I'm now initializing my two
environments for appctl.

00:14:44.190 --> 00:14:46.940
So I can start by adding
the staging environment

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:49.310
by specifying which
cluster and which

00:14:49.310 --> 00:14:52.940
namespace I want to deploy
my staging application in.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:58.080
And then I just do the same
thing for the prod environment.

00:14:58.080 --> 00:15:00.080
Once this is done,
both environments

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:03.080
are going to show up in
the Delivery directory.

00:15:03.080 --> 00:15:05.450
Here you can see just the
definition of the production

00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:06.870
environment.

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:11.390
Now the Config directory is a
customized config directory.

00:15:11.390 --> 00:15:14.060
And you can see that appctl
has provided the skeleton

00:15:14.060 --> 00:15:15.620
for my environments.

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:18.620
To make things simpler, I'm
just going to copy an existing

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:20.090
configuration.

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:24.470
And you can see that now
I have a deployment, where

00:15:24.470 --> 00:15:30.020
I don't specify the image
type tag and a service file,

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:31.310
where I create [INAUDIBLE].

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:34.530
And in the customization file
for my base configuration,

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:38.160
I am going to modify the
tag that I'm going to use.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:41.720
I'm just going to use v.0.0.1.

00:15:41.720 --> 00:15:43.810
And I'm referencing the
resources deployment

00:15:43.810 --> 00:15:44.930
[INAUDIBLE].

00:15:44.930 --> 00:15:49.240
And now for each environment,
I am modifying the message.

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:51.515
I'm giving a custom message.

00:15:51.515 --> 00:15:53.560
Here you can see for
the staging environment,

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:55.990
I'm seeing "Hello,
[INAUDIBLE] world."

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:59.620
Now let's commit tag and push
everything to the GitLab.

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:07.815
And obviously, this is going
to trigger a CI pipeline.

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:13.370
That CI pipeline has
several stages, including

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:15.680
one with three different tests.

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:18.800
And if we looked at, for
example, the unit test

00:16:18.800 --> 00:16:21.680
or in this case,
the linting, we are

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:24.980
going to see that GitLab
detects some problems

00:16:24.980 --> 00:16:29.040
with [INAUDIBLE],, which we don't
care too much about right now.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:31.940
And here, you can see the unit
tests that have all passed.

00:16:31.940 --> 00:16:33.930
That is important.

00:16:33.930 --> 00:16:36.800
But you can see that
there's a third test that

00:16:36.800 --> 00:16:38.330
hasn't succeeded.

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:39.950
And that's the Validate stage.

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:41.480
And what does that stage do?

00:16:41.480 --> 00:16:44.720
As you can see, we have
here policy validation,

00:16:44.720 --> 00:16:46.675
the same one that
we saw earlier,

00:16:46.675 --> 00:16:48.800
where it says that all
deployments must have a Team

00:16:48.800 --> 00:16:49.850
label.

00:16:49.850 --> 00:16:51.250
Where does that come from?

00:16:51.250 --> 00:16:54.320
It actually comes from
the previous stage,

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:57.050
where we downloaded the
policies from the Anthos Config

00:16:57.050 --> 00:16:59.030
Management repository.

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:01.040
And how do I do that?

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:04.200
If we take a look at
my GitLab CI YAML file,

00:17:04.200 --> 00:17:07.819
you can see that I actually
include another GitLab CI YAML

00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:09.920
file that comes from the
Anthos Config Management.

00:17:09.920 --> 00:17:12.710
So the platform team has
provided me [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:12.710 --> 00:17:15.829
with everything that I need to
check that my application is

00:17:15.829 --> 00:17:19.530
compliant with their policies.

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:21.680
So let's fix the
policy variation

00:17:21.680 --> 00:17:27.510
by adding this famous
Team label now.

00:17:27.510 --> 00:17:30.020
Label Team, team
HelloWorld-- we don't

00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:32.760
care too much about
getting value of the label.

00:17:32.760 --> 00:17:35.120
And now let's just
update the tag.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:42.280
And once again, commit
tag and push everything.

00:17:42.280 --> 00:17:45.460
Of course, this creates a new
CI pipeline and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:45.460 --> 00:17:48.940
This one is going
to actually succeed.

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:51.000
While we're waiting for
that pipeline to finish,

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:54.785
let's use appctl
to actually deploy

00:17:54.785 --> 00:17:55.910
in the staging environment.

00:17:55.910 --> 00:17:58.180
So here, I'm using the
appctl prepare command.

00:17:58.180 --> 00:18:02.230
And I'm seeing that they want
to deploy the tag with 0.0.2

00:18:02.230 --> 00:18:04.210
to the staging environment.

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:05.180
And what does that do?

00:18:05.180 --> 00:18:10.090
It actually creates a merge
request on my over repository,

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:14.310
if you remember, the
HelloWorld deployment one.

00:18:14.310 --> 00:18:18.330
So let's take a look
at that merge request.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:20.100
Here, you can
notice that I am not

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.480
in the repository for my
application [INAUDIBLE]

00:18:22.480 --> 00:18:24.690
HelloWorld deployment
repository.

00:18:24.690 --> 00:18:27.630
That's the one that
contains the manifests

00:18:27.630 --> 00:18:29.220
that I'm going to deploy.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:31.770
And that merge request
is a merge request

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:38.050
to add the fully [INAUDIBLE]
manifest to this repository.

00:18:38.050 --> 00:18:41.490
And as you can see, here in this
manifest, I have an image tag.

00:18:41.490 --> 00:18:43.200
And I have my custom message.

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:46.520
And I have moved my deployment
and service in the same thing.

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:48.270
And you can see that
I am actually merging

00:18:48.270 --> 00:18:49.800
into the staging branch.

00:18:49.800 --> 00:18:52.710
So appctl uses one
branch per environment.

00:18:52.710 --> 00:18:54.480
And the name of the
branch is the name

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:56.940
of the environment [INAUDIBLE].

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:00.840
So I just merged
that merge request.

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:04.530
And in parallel, the CI pipeline
on the HelloWorld repository

00:19:04.530 --> 00:19:05.100
has finished.

00:19:05.100 --> 00:19:07.200
And we actually
pushed the new image

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:10.230
to our container registry.

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:12.750
And now we just have
to use the appctl apply

00:19:12.750 --> 00:19:16.140
staging command to actually
deploy our application

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:17.760
to the staging environment.

00:19:17.760 --> 00:19:19.530
That command is
going to take what

00:19:19.530 --> 00:19:22.250
exists in the staging branch
of the HelloWorld deployment

00:19:22.250 --> 00:19:26.085
repository and is going to
apply it to our cluster.

00:19:31.840 --> 00:19:34.300
So this is apply command
takes a little bit of time.

00:19:34.300 --> 00:19:36.510
But once it's
finished, we actually

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:39.330
have a new application
in the Applications tab

00:19:39.330 --> 00:19:41.130
of Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:19:41.130 --> 00:19:43.350
Here you can see my
HelloWorld application

00:19:43.350 --> 00:19:45.890
in the Staging cluster.

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:47.250
For now, it's empty.

00:19:47.250 --> 00:19:49.350
But very soon, we
are going to see

00:19:49.350 --> 00:19:52.710
the different components
as [INAUDIBLE] service

00:19:52.710 --> 00:19:54.510
and deployment.

00:19:54.510 --> 00:19:57.240
And you can see that we
have very specific version

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:00.310
of that application
that is [INAUDIBLE]..

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:04.200
And if we look at our
HelloWorld namespace,

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:06.570
you're going to see the
HelloWorld deployment and a job

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:09.720
that has deployed [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:13.830
And now, we can just look at
the HelloWorld service and at

00:20:13.830 --> 00:20:14.730
[INAUDIBLE].

00:20:14.730 --> 00:20:16.530
This is just a
little bit sped up

00:20:16.530 --> 00:20:21.480
because we are waiting for
the [INAUDIBLE] to come in.

00:20:21.480 --> 00:20:23.690
While waiting for the
[INAUDIBLE] to come in,

00:20:23.690 --> 00:20:26.695
we are going to actually prepare
deployment to production.

00:20:26.695 --> 00:20:30.800
And in that case, we are not
going to deploy from a tag.

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:33.320
But we're going to deploy
from the staging environment,

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:36.050
meaning that we're going to
promote a diversion that we

00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:38.690
have in staging to the
production environment.

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:43.250
And it's just the from-env
parameter instead of from-tag.

00:20:43.250 --> 00:20:46.820
And like before, it creates a
merge request in the HelloWorld

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:49.480
deployment repository,
in the one that contains

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:51.105
my [INAUDIBLE] configuration.

00:20:51.105 --> 00:20:52.760
And in that case, we
are going to merge

00:20:52.760 --> 00:20:55.820
onto the production branch,
the prod branch and not

00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:57.470
the staging one.

00:20:57.470 --> 00:20:59.600
And in my fully
[INAUDIBLE] manifest,

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:02.640
I'm indeed using the same
version of my application.

00:21:02.640 --> 00:21:05.610
But now I have a
different custom message.

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:07.100
So it's merged.

00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:08.600
And in the meantime,
the [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:09.683
probably has been created.

00:21:11.975 --> 00:21:12.475
Yes.

00:21:12.475 --> 00:21:15.960
Here, you can see it with
the staging custom message.

00:21:15.960 --> 00:21:18.800
Now let's apply our newly
updated configuration

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:20.972
to the prod environment.

00:21:20.972 --> 00:21:22.430
And that's exactly
the same process

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:24.530
as for the staging environment.

00:21:24.530 --> 00:21:29.750
Appctl is going to take
the tip of the prod branch

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:32.818
in the HelloWorld deployment
repository and apply it.

00:21:32.818 --> 00:21:35.360
And we're going to have a new
application in the applications

00:21:35.360 --> 00:21:37.640
page of GKE.

00:21:37.640 --> 00:21:42.750
And it's going to be exactly the
same thing as for the staging.

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:45.260
Here we have our
new application.

00:21:45.260 --> 00:21:47.180
And let's take a
look at the service

00:21:47.180 --> 00:21:48.600
for that new application.

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:52.497
It's, of course, in the
HelloWorld namespace,

00:21:52.497 --> 00:21:53.330
in the prod cluster.

00:21:56.020 --> 00:21:59.260
And let's refresh to get the
actual IP of the service.

00:22:06.630 --> 00:22:11.180
And here, we have service with
the production custom message.

00:22:11.180 --> 00:22:12.430
MAYANK TAHILRAMANI: Brilliant.

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:13.820
Theo, thanks for that demo.

00:22:13.820 --> 00:22:18.360
Now that we've seen the
solution live and in the demo,

00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:20.540
let's take a half step
back to really recap

00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:22.135
some key takeaways.

00:22:22.135 --> 00:22:23.510
Really answer that
question-- why

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:25.850
GitLab and Anthos for GitOps?

00:22:25.850 --> 00:22:28.490
As mentioned earlier, Google
and GitLab partnering together

00:22:28.490 --> 00:22:30.500
to really lower that
barrier of entry

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:32.390
into the GitOps
workflows, particularly

00:22:32.390 --> 00:22:35.480
for those companies that are
standardizing on Kubernetes,

00:22:35.480 --> 00:22:37.730
trying to manage their
complexity, trying to software

00:22:37.730 --> 00:22:41.240
define their business to be
more iterative and agile.

00:22:41.240 --> 00:22:43.760
As they explore
best practices, Git

00:22:43.760 --> 00:22:45.650
becomes a fundamental
building block

00:22:45.650 --> 00:22:48.560
to kind of provide
that audit trail,

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:50.180
provide that coherent
[? cohesity ?]

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:51.780
of the single source of truth.

00:22:51.780 --> 00:22:56.330
Using GitLab as a single
application just beyond Git--

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:58.520
with the approval workflows
with merge requests,

00:22:58.520 --> 00:23:00.890
automation pipeline-- you
got a single application

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:03.590
with the user interface
and user experience

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:06.490
for all the different
users and organization BUs

00:23:06.490 --> 00:23:10.520
are invested in continually
improving the business

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:12.380
through software-defined means.

00:23:12.380 --> 00:23:16.490
Why limit declared management
to app and only infrastructure?

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:19.520
Look into policy management
as well as Theo demonstrated

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:21.530
with Anthos Config Management.

00:23:21.530 --> 00:23:24.650
Use the power of Git to manage
your configs and policies

00:23:24.650 --> 00:23:27.890
while enforcing strong
security posture.

00:23:27.890 --> 00:23:29.720
Really with GitLab
and Anthos, you

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:32.000
get a full developer
experiential, full DevOps

00:23:32.000 --> 00:23:33.820
experience in a
unified platform.

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:36.560
You standardize on a single
DevOps tool and a single DevOps

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:40.220
application, on top of an
open and unified platform

00:23:40.220 --> 00:23:42.522
that your company
can adopt quickly.

00:23:42.522 --> 00:23:44.480
At this point, you're
probably wondering, well,

00:23:44.480 --> 00:23:45.560
how do you get started?

00:23:45.560 --> 00:23:46.830
Where to begin?

00:23:46.830 --> 00:23:50.570
Great news is we are
partnering with SADA and Arctic

00:23:50.570 --> 00:23:52.430
as channel partners
that can help guide you

00:23:52.430 --> 00:23:54.320
through that process
and really help you

00:23:54.320 --> 00:23:56.900
adopt what you saw here today.

00:23:56.900 --> 00:23:58.780
Alternatively, we do
have reference material

00:23:58.780 --> 00:24:00.470
that you can check out offline.

00:24:00.470 --> 00:24:03.380
We've got two solution guides
that are readily available

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:06.500
today, where you can
see and read more

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:09.560
about the best practices on how
to architect that application

00:24:09.560 --> 00:24:11.240
repository that
Theo was mentioning,

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:13.310
where you store
your config policies

00:24:13.310 --> 00:24:15.350
for your multiple clusters.

00:24:15.350 --> 00:24:17.670
This is a solution paper
that Theo actually wrote.

00:24:17.670 --> 00:24:20.370
So definitely check it out to
get more finer detail on how

00:24:20.370 --> 00:24:22.790
you build up that repository.

00:24:22.790 --> 00:24:26.660
Additionally, we've got
another solution paper live now

00:24:26.660 --> 00:24:31.700
that helps you and outlines how
you can deploy GitLab on prem

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:35.312
on a GKE on-prem cluster that
may be managed by Anthos.

00:24:35.312 --> 00:24:36.770
So if you're looking
to get started

00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:39.312
on a hybrid environment, that
might be a great solution paper

00:24:39.312 --> 00:24:39.923
to check out.

00:24:39.923 --> 00:24:41.840
Additionally, we've got
a lot of documentation

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:44.720
available as well on Anthos
Config Management in general

00:24:44.720 --> 00:24:48.140
on Google's Docs page,
as well as finer details

00:24:48.140 --> 00:24:50.900
on the appctl utility
on how to use it.

00:24:50.900 --> 00:24:53.060
We've got documentation
on GitLab,

